  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**What the data holds:** the data view has about 14,000 web requests timestamped between 4 Oct and 3 Dec 2026, so much of it is dated in the future. Each request records the response code, bytes sent, client IP, URL, source and destination country, operating system, file extension and host. Only three response codes appear: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n\n**Dashboard `EFF-02bf062f Web logs overview`** has 7 panels:\n- Single-number panels for total requests, unique visitors (counted by client IP) and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of each response code's share of requests.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and bytes.\n- The top 10 countries requests come from.\n\nBecause the data runs into December, I set the dashboard's default time range to the last 7 days through 60 days from now. With a \"last 7 days\" range, most of the data would be hidden.\n\n**Rule `EFF-02bf062f 5xx spike`** (id `38e2a725-dad8-44cf-9a83-5e491a5bcf0d`) checks `kibana_sample_data_logs` every minute. It fires when more than 10 requests with a 5xx code arrive within 5 minutes, using the `timestamp` field for time. It finds 5xx codes by looking for responses that start with \"5\"; I confirmed this matches the 441 requests with code 503. The rule has no actions attached, so it records alerts in Kibana but doesn't send notifications.\n\nDashboard: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-02bf062f-web-logs-overview-344752b665ac",
